%N A402648 Decimal expansion of the optimal slope Λ in the spinless modular bootstrap bound Δ_1 <= c/Λ + o(c) on the lightest non-vacuum primary of a unitary, compact 2D CFT with no extra conserved currents.
%C A402648 That is, the decimal expansion of the limit as c increases without bound of c/Delta*(c). Here Delta*(c) is the best upper bound on Delta_1 that the spinless modular bootstrap can prove at central charge c, and Delta_1 is the scaling dimension of the lightest non-vacuum primary. "Spinless modular bootstrap" means applying linear functionals, built as finite combinations of derivatives, to the S-invariance of the torus partition function at zero angular potential. The bound has to hold for every unitary, compact 2D CFT with c > 1 and no conserved currents beyond the Virasoro algebra.
%C A402648 This constant is transcendental. If it were algebraic, log(1+sqrt(2))/Pi would be algebraic, and then 1+sqrt(2) = (-1)^(-i*log(1+sqrt(2))/Pi) would be transcendental by the Gelfond-Schneider theorem, a contradiction.
%C A402648 Hellerman (2009) proved the slope is at least 6. Hartman, Mazac, & Rastelli (2019) improved the slope analytically to 8.503. Afkhami-Jeddi, Hartman, & Tajdini (2019) gave numerical evidence that the slope is around 9.1.
